### Plant-pollinator interactions and plant abundance

Following the same protocol at the study locality as published in @Janovsky2013, we collected data on pollinator visitation activity. We used same permanent plots as in the flowering plant abundance to record all interactions of flower-visiting pollinators with flowering *Succisa pratensis* and other plant species in the surrounding of *S. pratensis*. All flowers within the plot were checked only one time to avoid pseudo-replication and all interactions occurring at the moment of the survey were recorded, lasting up to five minutes. Each plot was recorded at least twenty times during the activity period of pollinators (7:00 - 19:00) at the peak of flowering (mid-August) each year. We recorded only the pollinators touching the reproductive organs of the visited plant and each individual pollinator only once. Finally, we counted standardized interaction rates (total number of interactions per sampling visit) for pollinators visiting S*. pratensis* from 2022 to 2024 and compared the distribution of interaction rates for the specific group of pollinators visiting *S. pratensis* and the same pollinators on all other plant species.

Thanks to long-term research on flowering plants and their interactions with pollinators, we obtained information on the abundances of individual plants at the study site and it’s changes over time (see Janovský et al., 2013). We used 4 × 4 m2 permanent plots each placed in the center of each grid point of a 20 × 20 m2 grid covering a total area of 300 × 300 m2 of the study site. To determine the floral neighborhood of *S. pratensis* from 2022 to 2024, we counted distribution of plant abundance for individual plots where *S. pratensis* was present. We counted conspecific abundance of the focal species and heterospecific co-flowering abundance index, which is the cumulative abundance of all other co-flowering plant species in the plot.

### Pollen load

Foraging hoverflies visiting Succisa inflorescence were caught with entomological net and killed immediately after capture. We swabbed pollen grains from one half (randomly selecting left or right) of each pollinator’s body with a small block of fuchsin jelly, following @beattie1971technique. We stored captured pollinators in the 70% ethanol. On the same day, we produced semi-permanent samples by melting fuchsin jelly with a heating pad on a microscope slide, covering and sealing with nail polish. Later, we counted and determined all pollen grains using a light microscope. We sorted pollen into conspecific *S. pratensis* pollen and heterospecific pollen of other plant species.

### Single Visit Deposition (SVD)

To prevent pollinators access to flowers, we bagged selected inflorences into a tulle bag before the plants have entered the male phase and opened the anthers. When the plant entered the female phase with receptive stigmas, we removed the bag and collected two control stigmas into a tube with 70% ethanol. We watched the inflorescence for thirty minutes and recorded every pollinator that visited it. The pollinators were allowed to visit only one flower within the inflorescence, the stigma of which we collected into a tube with 70% ethanol. If the pollinator visited multiple flowers, we collected only the first visited stigma and removed other from the plant.

### Total Pollen Deposition (TPD) on Stigma

We put collected stigmas on the slide and let the ethanol evaporate on the heating pad. After that we added 1 µl of 10% KOH, let the samples dry, added 3 µl of 3% hydrogen peroxide, let the samples dry again and we added 13 µl of 8% vinegar to neutralize KOH. After further drying we added 5 µl of dye (safranin, water and glycerol), covered the samples with a coverslip, applied gentle, even pressure on the cover glass to squash the stigma and sealed with nail polish to produce semi-permanent samples.

### Statistical Analysis

#### Variance partitioning

#### Monte Carlo Simulation of Visitation Redundancy

To test whether visitation redundancy mathematically drives stable pollen deposition, we constructed a Monte Carlo bootstrapping simulation. Because pollinator morphology and mechanical transfer efficiency are functionally consistent traits, we pooled all single-visit deposition (SVD) data across the three study years to create a single, highly robust global pool of vector deposition profiles. This allowed us to mathematically isolate the effect of the plant's changing floral density while holding vector baseline deposition constant.

We parameterized the simulation independently for each study year (2022–2024). For a given year, we estimated the average required visitation rate by dividing the empirical mean of conspecific pollen (CP) on that year's stigmas by the mean CP of the global SVD pool. We then generated 10,000 virtual stigmas per year, randomly sampling (with replacement) from the global SVD pool based on that year's specific visitation rate.

Finally, we evaluated the fit of the simulated null models against our true field observations. Because the massive sample size of the simulation (N=10,000) renders traditional two-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov (K-S) p-values hyper-sensitive to trivial biological deviations—such as physical stigma saturation limits—we focused on the K-S D-statistic to quantify the maximum distributional divergence as a measure of effect size. Furthermore, to provide a robust and intuitive measure of simulation accuracy independent of sample size, we calculated the Overlapping Coefficient (OVL; Pastore & Calcagnì, 2019). The OVL quantifies the exact proportion of area shared between the empirical and simulated probability density curves, providing a direct metric of functional overlap between the theoretical null model and field reality.

## Results

### Plant-pollinator interactions and plant abundance

To determine if the 2024 high-density anomaly altered the mechanical interaction between pollinators and the focal plant population, we modeled the per-capita visitation rate as a function of local floral abundance. A log-log linear regression revealed a significant negative relationship (t=−2.75, p=0.008, R2=0.12). As local conspecific floral abundance increased, the per-capita visitation rate per flower significantly decreased. This confirms a density-driven dilution effect: while the massive floral bloom successfully recruited shared generalist vectors, the sheer volume of available conspecific resources diluted the frequency of individual visits, trapping vectors within a pure conspecific foraging loop for extended periods.

Decoupled Accumulation: The Spatial Mitigation of HP
To test if this spatial dilution effectively mitigated heterospecific pollen (HP) transfer, we analyzed the accumulation rates of HP relative to conspecific pollen (CP) receipt on final stigmas. An analysis of variance (ANOVA) revealed a highly significant interaction between CP accumulation and study year (F2,231​=13.38, p<0.001). In standard-density years (2022 and 2023), the accumulation of CP was tightly coupled with heavy HP contamination, resulting in steep, positive slopes. In these years, receiving more conspecific visits inevitably resulted in higher reproductive interference from the shared network. However, during the extreme high-density anomaly of 2024, this relationship structurally shifted. The accumulation slope flattened significantly (CP × Year 2024 interaction: t=−2.44, p=0.015), demonstrating that stigmas could accumulate their required baseline of CP without the proportional accumulation of HP. This confirms that extreme local floral density actively decouples conspecific reproduction from heterospecific contamination in the field.

Plant abundance shift and the effect on visitation - needs to be tested.

```{r}
#| label: fig-spatial-temporal-abundance
#| fig-cap: "Distribution and abundance of Succisa pratensis (2022-2024). A) Spatial distribution of plots with Succisa presence. B) Total abundance recorded per year. C) Differences in standardized visitation rates to Succisa pratensis across years (2022-2024)."
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| fig-height: 8
# --- PART A: MAP ---

# 1. Prepare Presence Data
succisa_presence <- plant_abundance %>%
    filter(year %in% c(22, 23, 24)) %>%
    select(year, plot_id, Suc_pra) %>%
    filter(Suc_pra > 0) %>%
    mutate(
        year_label = paste0("20", year),
        plot_id_join = as.character(as.integer(plot_id))
    )

# 2. Prepare Spatial Data
plots_sf <- hanpolnet_plots %>%
    mutate(
        plot_id_join = ifelse(grepl("^[0-9]+$", plot_id),
            as.character(as.integer(plot_id)),
            plot_id
        )
    )

# 3. Join
map_data <- succisa_presence %>%
    inner_join(plots_sf, by = "plot_id_join") %>%
    st_as_sf()

# 4. Create Map Plot Object
p_map <- ggplot() +
    geom_sf(data = plots_sf, color = "grey85", size = 1.5, shape = 1) +
    geom_sf(data = map_data, aes(color = year_label), size = 2) +
    facet_wrap(~year_label) +
    scale_color_viridis_d(begin = 0.2, end = 0.8, option = "D") +
    theme_classic() +
    labs(
        title = "Succisa pratensis Presence",
        subtitle = "Plots with recorded abundance > 0",
        color = "Year"
    ) +
    theme(
        axis.text = element_blank(),
        axis.title = element_blank(),
        panel.grid = element_blank(),
        legend.position = "right"
    )

# --- PART B: ABUNDANCE ---

# 1. Summarize
abundance_summary <- plant_abundance %>%
    filter(year %in% c(22, 23, 24)) %>%
    group_by(year) %>%
    summarise(total_abundance = sum(Suc_pra, na.rm = TRUE)) %>%
    mutate(year_label = paste0("20", year))

# 2. Create Barplot Object
p_abundance <- ggplot(abundance_summary, aes(x = year_label, y = total_abundance, fill = year_label)) +
    geom_col(alpha = 0.8, width = 0.6) +
    labs(
        title = "Total Succisa pratensis Abundance",
        x = "Year",
        y = "Total Abundance\n (flowering stalks)"
    ) +
    scale_fill_viridis_d(begin = 0.2, end = 0.8, option = "D") +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(legend.position = "none")

# --- PART C: VISITATION ---

# Sum rates per plot to get total visitation intensity per plot
visitation_per_plot <- interaction_data_Succisa_22_24 %>%
    group_by(year, plot_id) %>%
    summarise(total_rate = sum(rate, na.rm = TRUE), .groups = "drop") %>%
    filter(!is.na(year)) %>%
    mutate(year = factor(paste0("20", year)))


# Plot
p_visitation <- ggplot(visitation_per_plot, aes(x = year, y = total_rate, fill = year)) +
    geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.6, outlier.shape = NA) +
    geom_jitter(width = 0.2, alpha = 0.5) +
    labs(
        title = "Visitation Rates per Plot",
        x = "Year",
        y = "Standardized Visitation Rate\n (interactions/sampling effort)"
    ) +
    scale_fill_viridis_d(begin = 0.3, end = 0.7) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(legend.position = "none")

# --- PART D: VISITATION PER PLANT ABUNDACE ---

# Sum rates per plot to get total visitation intensity per plot
plant_abundance_per_plot <- plant_abundance_22_24 %>%
    group_by(year, plot_id) %>%
    summarise(plot_abundance = sum(Suc_pra, na.rm = TRUE), .groups = "drop") %>%
    filter(!is.na(year)) %>%
    mutate(
        year = factor(paste0("20", year)),
        plot_id = as.character(plot_id)
    )

visitation_per_plant <- visitation_per_plot %>%
    left_join(plant_abundance_per_plot, by = c("year", "plot_id")) %>%
    mutate(rate_per_plant = ifelse(plot_abundance > 0, total_rate / plot_abundance, NA))


# Plot
p_visitation_per_plant <- ggplot(visitation_per_plant, aes(x = year, y = rate_per_plant, fill = year)) +
    geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.6, outlier.shape = NA) +
    geom_jitter(width = 0.2, alpha = 0.5) +
    labs(
        title = "Visitation Rates per Plant",
        x = "Year",
        y = "Standardized Visitation Rate\n (interactions/sampling effort/plant abundance)"
    ) +
    scale_fill_viridis_d(begin = 0.3, end = 0.7) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(legend.position = "none")


# --- COMBINE ---
ggarrange(p_abundance, p_visitation, p_visitation_per_plant,
    ncol = 1,
    labels = c("A", "B", "C"),
    heights = c(2, 2, 2)
)
```

### Pollinator Spectra Composition

To assess the temporal stability of the pollinator community, we compared species composition across years using the **Morisita-Horn similarity index** (Horn, 1966). This metric was selected for its robustness to differences in sample size and species richness compared to incidence-based metrics (Jost et al., 2011). Pairwise similarities were calculated on abundance data (standardized by sampling effort) using the `vegan` package.

The core structure of the pollinator community remained highly stable across the three years. The Morisita-Horn index indicated high compositional overlap (Similarity \> 0.9 for all pairwise comparisons), driven by the consistent dominance of key generalist taxa such as *Eristalis* spp. and *Syrphus* spp. (see Table S3 in Supplementary Material).

```{r}
#| label: analysis-community-stability-evenness
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| fig-cap: "Community Stability and Structure. A) Pollinator Spectra Composition. B) Floral Spectra Composition. C) Temporal Pollinator Stability (Morisita-Horn). D) Floral Community Similarity."
#| fig-height: 10
#| fig-width: 14
# --- Part 0: Pollinator Spectra Composition ---
species_composition <- interaction_data_Succisa_22_24 %>%
    filter(!is.na(year)) %>%
    group_by(year, pollinator_id) %>%
    summarise(total_rate = sum(rate, na.rm = TRUE), .groups = "drop") %>%
    group_by(year) %>%
    mutate(
        yearly_total = sum(total_rate),
        proportion = total_rate / yearly_total,
        year_label = paste0("20", year)
    )

top_n_species <- 9
top_species_list <- species_composition %>%
    group_by(pollinator_id) %>%
    summarise(grand_total = sum(total_rate)) %>%
    arrange(desc(grand_total)) %>%
    slice_head(n = top_n_species) %>%
    pull(pollinator_id)

species_composition_grouped <- species_composition %>%
    mutate(
        species_label = ifelse(pollinator_id %in% top_species_list,
            pollinator_id,
            "Other"
        )
    ) %>%
    group_by(year_label, species_label) %>%
    summarise(proportion = sum(proportion), .groups = "drop")

species_composition_grouped$species_label <- factor(
    species_composition_grouped$species_label,
    levels = c("Other", rev(top_species_list))
)

pollinator_colors <- c(
    "Eristalis_tenax" = "#30123B", "Eristalis_pertinax" = "#4675ED",
    "Eristalis_interrupta" = "#39568C", "Eristalis_arbustorum" = "#42F4EB",
    "Eristalis_intricaria" = "#1E9C89", "Eristalis_similis" = "#248641",
    "Eristalis_sp" = "#248641", "Helophilus_pendulus" = "#A2FC3C",
    "Helophilus_trivittatus" = "#D9F121", "Helophilus_hybridus" = "#D9F121",
    "Myathropa_florea" = "#F9B614", "Apis_mellifera" = "#F9B614",
    "Bombus_lapidarius" = "#EB520E", "Sarcophaga_sp" = "#A42C17",
    "Zygena_sp" = "#D2204C", "Sericomyia" = "#D2204C",
    "Tachinidae" = "grey50", "Other" = "grey80"
)

p_composition <- ggplot(species_composition_grouped, aes(x = year_label, y = proportion, fill = species_label)) +
    geom_col(width = 0.6, color = "white", size = 0.2) +
    scale_fill_manual(values = pollinator_colors, name = "Species") +
    labs(
        title = "A. Pollinator Spectra Composition",
        subtitle = "Proportion of Visitation Rates",
        x = "Year",
        y = "Proportion"
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        legend.position = "right",
        legend.text = element_text(face = "italic")
    )

# --- Part 1: Community Similarity (Heatmap) ---

# 1. Aggregation for Stability Analysis
# Sum standardized rates per species per year, excluding NA years
community_matrix <- interaction_data_Succisa_22_24 %>%
    filter(!is.na(year)) %>%
    group_by(year, pollinator_id) %>%
    summarise(abundance = sum(rate, na.rm = TRUE), .groups = "drop") %>%
    pivot_wider(names_from = pollinator_id, values_from = abundance, values_fill = 0)

# Extract Matrix (Species columns only)
comm_mat <- as.matrix(community_matrix[, -1])
# Label clearly as 2022, 2023, 2024
rownames(comm_mat) <- paste0("20", community_matrix$year)

# 2. Calculate Morisita-Horn Dissimilarity
# vegdist returns dissimilarity (0 = identical, 1 = different)
mh_dist <- vegdist(comm_mat, method = "horn")

# Convert to Similarity (1 - Dissimilarity)
mh_sim <- 1 - as.matrix(mh_dist)

# 3. Visualization: Correlogram (Styled Heatmap)
# Mask upper triangle to create a lower-triangular plot
mh_sim[upper.tri(mh_sim)] <- NA

# Reshape for ggplot
mh_long <- as.data.frame(mh_sim) %>%
    mutate(Year1 = rownames(.)) %>%
    pivot_longer(cols = -Year1, names_to = "Year2", values_to = "Similarity") %>%
    filter(!is.na(Similarity))

p_similarity <- ggplot(mh_long, aes(x = Year2, y = Year1, fill = Similarity)) +
    geom_tile(color = "white") +
    geom_text(aes(label = sprintf("%.2f", Similarity)), color = "black", size = 5) +
    # Red-Blue gradient (0 = Red, 1 = Blue)
    scale_fill_gradient2(
        low = "#D73027", mid = "#FFFFBF", high = "#4575B4",
        midpoint = 0.5, limit = c(0, 1), name = "Similarity"
    ) +
    # Reverse Y axis to put first year on top (Matrix layout)
    scale_y_discrete(limits = rev) +
    labs(
        title = "C. Pollinator Composition Similarity",
        subtitle = "Morisita-Horn Similarity Index",
        x = "",
        y = ""
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        axis.text = element_text(size = 12, face = "bold"),
        panel.grid = element_blank(),
        legend.position = "right"
    )

# --- Part 2: Floral Community Similarity (Heatmap) ---

# 1. Aggregation for Stability Analysis
# Ensure proper year format
plant_matrix_prep <- plant_abundance_22_24 %>%
    mutate(year = as.numeric(as.character(year))) %>%
    mutate(year = if_else(year < 100, year + 2000, year)) %>%
    # Sum abundance per species per year (across plots)
    # The abundance data is already standardized per species/plot, so summing gives a community-level profile
    pivot_longer(cols = -c(year, plot_id), names_to = "Species", values_to = "Abundance") %>%
    group_by(year, Species) %>%
    summarise(total_abundance = sum(Abundance, na.rm = TRUE), .groups = "drop") %>%
    pivot_wider(names_from = Species, values_from = total_abundance, values_fill = 0)

# Extract Matrix
if (nrow(plant_matrix_prep) > 0) {
    comm_mat_plant <- as.matrix(plant_matrix_prep[, -1])
    rownames(comm_mat_plant) <- as.character(plant_matrix_prep$year)

    # 2. Calculate Morisita-Horn
    mh_dist_plant <- vegdist(comm_mat_plant, method = "horn")
    mh_sim_plant <- 1 - as.matrix(mh_dist_plant)

    # 3. Visualization
    mh_sim_plant[upper.tri(mh_sim_plant)] <- NA

    mh_long_plant <- as.data.frame(mh_sim_plant) %>%
        mutate(Year1 = rownames(.)) %>%
        pivot_longer(cols = -Year1, names_to = "Year2", values_to = "Similarity") %>%
        filter(!is.na(Similarity))

    p_plant_similarity <- ggplot(mh_long_plant, aes(x = Year2, y = Year1, fill = Similarity)) +
        geom_tile(color = "white") +
        geom_text(aes(label = sprintf("%.2f", Similarity)), color = "black", size = 5) +
        # Conifer Green to Brown Gradient
        # High (1.0) = #2E8651 (Conifer Green)
        # Mid (0.5) = #FFF8DC (Cornsilk/Beige)
        # Low (0.0) = #8B4513 (SaddleBrown)
        scale_fill_gradient2(
            low = "#8B4513", mid = "#FFF8DC", high = "#2E8651",
            midpoint = 0.5, limit = c(0, 1), name = "Similarity"
        ) +
        scale_y_discrete(limits = rev) +
        labs(
            title = "D. Floral Community Similarity",
            subtitle = "Morisita-Horn Similarity Index",
            x = "",
            y = ""
        ) +
        theme_classic() +
        theme(
            axis.text = element_text(size = 12, face = "bold"),
            panel.grid = element_blank(),
            legend.position = "right"
        )
} else {
    p_plant_similarity <- ggplot() +
        labs(title = "No Plant Data Available")
}

# --- Part 3: Floral Spectra Composition ---
plant_composition_grouped <- plant_matrix_prep %>%
    pivot_longer(cols = -year, names_to = "Species", values_to = "total_abundance") %>%
    filter(total_abundance > 0) %>%
    group_by(year) %>%
    mutate(
        yearly_total = sum(total_abundance),
        proportion = total_abundance / yearly_total,
        year_label = as.character(year)
    ) %>%
    ungroup()

top_n_plants <- 9
top_plant_list <- plant_composition_grouped %>%
    group_by(Species) %>%
    summarise(grand_total = sum(total_abundance)) %>%
    arrange(desc(grand_total)) %>%
    slice_head(n = top_n_plants) %>%
    pull(Species)

plant_composition_grouped <- plant_composition_grouped %>%
    mutate(species_label = ifelse(Species %in% top_plant_list, Species, "Other")) %>%
    group_by(year_label, species_label) %>%
    summarise(proportion = sum(proportion), .groups = "drop")

plant_composition_grouped$species_label <- factor(
    plant_composition_grouped$species_label,
    levels = c("Other", rev(top_plant_list))
)

p_plant_composition <- ggplot(plant_composition_grouped, aes(x = year_label, y = proportion, fill = species_label)) +
    geom_col(width = 0.6, color = "white", linewidth = 0.2) +
    scale_fill_viridis_d(option = "H", direction = 1, name = "Species") +
    labs(
        title = "B. Floral Spectra Composition",
        subtitle = "Proportion of Plant Abundance",
        x = "Year",
        y = "Proportion"
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        legend.position = "right",
        legend.text = element_text(face = "italic")
    )


if (!requireNamespace("ggpubr", quietly = TRUE)) install.packages("ggpubr")

# Combine in Layout
# Row 1: A (Pollinator Spectra) | B (Floral Spectra)
# Row 2: C (Pollinator Sim) | D (Floral Sim)
row1 <- ggpubr::ggarrange(p_composition, p_plant_composition, ncol = 2, labels = NULL)
row2 <- ggpubr::ggarrange(p_similarity, p_plant_similarity, ncol = 2, labels = NULL)
ggpubr::ggarrange(row1, row2, nrow = 2, heights = c(1.5, 1))
```

### Pollen Load and Single Visit Deposition (SVD)

To determine if specific pollinator taxa drive the depositional variance observed across the network, we compared single-visit depositions (SVD) across the dominant floral visitor morphogroups (e.g., Eristalis spp., Helophilus spp., Bombus spp.). Kruskal-Wallis rank sum tests revealed no significant differences among pollinator taxa in the amount of conspecific pollen deposited during a single visit (H3​=1.59, p=0.661). Similarly, there were no significant differences across taxa in the deposition of heterospecific pollen per visit (H3​=5.20, p=0.158). These results indicate that per-visit pollen transfer efficiency and heterospecific carryover are highly stochastic but functionally equivalent across the generalist pollinator assemblage. Consequently, the total stigmatic pollen loads received by Succisa pratensis are not dictated by the taxonomic identity or morphological specialization of its visitors, but purely by the cumulative frequency of their visits.

```{r}
#| label: analysis-body-and-svd
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| fig-cap: "Pollen Load and Single Visit Deposition (SVD). A) Conspecific Body Pollen Load, B) Heterospecific Body Pollen Load, C) Conspecific Single Visit Deposition, D) Heterospecific Single Visit Deposition."
#| fig-height: 10
#| fig-width: 10
# 1. Data Cleaning & Grouping for Body Load
Body_clean <- pollen_load_guts_24 %>%
    rename(species = pollinator_species) %>%
    mutate(
        # Create Morphogroups identical to SVD
        morphogroup = case_when(
            grepl("Eristalis", species) ~ "Eristalis spp.",
            grepl("Bombus", species) ~ "Bombus spp.",
            species == "Apis mellifera" ~ "Apis mellifera",
            grepl("Syrphus", species) ~ "Syrphus spp.",
            grepl("Helophilus", species) ~ "Helophilus spp.",
            TRUE ~ "Other"
        )
    )

# Filter for groups with sufficient sample size (N >= 5) for visualization
group_counts_body <- Body_clean %>%
    count(morphogroup) %>%
    filter(n >= 5)
Body_final <- Body_clean %>%
    filter(morphogroup %in% group_counts_body$morphogroup)

# Panel A: Conspecific Pollen Load on Body
p_eff_body <- ggplot(Body_final, aes(x = morphogroup, y = suc_pra_load, fill = morphogroup)) +
    geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.6, outlier.shape = NA) +
    geom_jitter(width = 0.2, alpha = 0.5, size = 1.5) +
    ggpubr::stat_compare_means(method = "kruskal.test", label = "p.format",
        label.x.npc = "left", label.y.npc = 0.05, size = 3.5) +
    labs(
        title = "A. Conspecific Pollen (CP)",
        y = "Conspecific Pollen on Body",
        x = ""
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        axis.text.x = element_blank(),
        axis.title.x = element_blank(),
        axis.ticks.x = element_blank(),
        legend.position = "none"
    )

# Panel B: Heterospecific Pollen Load on Body
p_mess_body <- ggplot(Body_final, aes(x = morphogroup, y = other_pollen_load, fill = morphogroup)) +
    geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.6, outlier.shape = NA) +
    geom_jitter(width = 0.2, alpha = 0.5, size = 1.5) +
    ggpubr::stat_compare_means(method = "kruskal.test", label = "p.format",
        label.x.npc = "left", label.y.npc = 0.05, size = 3.5) +
    labs(
        title = "B. Heterospecific Pollen (HP)",
        y = "Heterospecific Pollen on Body",
        x = ""
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        axis.text.x = element_blank(),
        axis.title.x = element_blank(),
        axis.ticks.x = element_blank(),
        legend.position = "none"
    )

# 2. Data Cleaning & Grouping for SVD
SVD_clean <- SVD_Succisa_22_24 %>%
    rename(
        duration_sec = delka_navstevy,
        year = rok,
        species = pollinator_species
    ) %>%
    mutate(
        # Create Morphogroups
        morphogroup = case_when(
            grepl("Eristalis", species) ~ "Eristalis spp.",
            grepl("Bombus", species) ~ "Bombus spp.",
            species == "Apis mellifera" ~ "Apis mellifera",
            grepl("Syrphus", species) ~ "Syrphus spp.",
            grepl("Helophilus", species) ~ "Helophilus spp.",
            TRUE ~ "Other"
        )
    )

# Filter for groups with sufficient sample size (N > 5) for visualization
group_counts <- SVD_clean %>%
    count(morphogroup) %>%
    filter(n >= 5)
SVD_final <- SVD_clean %>%
    filter(morphogroup %in% group_counts$morphogroup)

# 3. Visualization for SVD

# Panel C: Efficiency (CP SVD)
p_eff <- ggplot(SVD_final, aes(x = morphogroup, y = conspecific_pollen, fill = morphogroup)) +
    geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.6, outlier.shape = NA) +
    geom_jitter(width = 0.2, alpha = 0.5, size = 1.5) +
    ggpubr::stat_compare_means(method = "kruskal.test", label = "p.format",
        label.x.npc = "left", label.y.npc = 0.05, size = 3.5) +
    labs(
        title = "C. Conspecific Pollen (CP)",
        y = "Conspecific Pollen SVD",
        x = "Pollinator Group"
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1, face = "italic"),
        legend.position = "none"
    )

# Panel D: Messiness (HP SVD)
p_mess <- ggplot(SVD_final, aes(x = morphogroup, y = heterospecific_pollen, fill = morphogroup)) +
    geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.6, outlier.shape = NA) +
    geom_jitter(width = 0.2, alpha = 0.5, size = 1.5) +
    ggpubr::stat_compare_means(method = "kruskal.test", label = "p.format",
        label.x.npc = "left", label.y.npc = 0.05, size = 3.5) +
    labs(
        title = "D. Heterospecific Pollen (HP)",
        y = "Heterospecific Pollen SVD",
        x = "Pollinator Group"
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1, face = "italic"),
        legend.position = "none"
    )

# Combine both using patchwork
if (!requireNamespace("patchwork", quietly = TRUE)) install.packages("patchwork")
library(patchwork)
p_combined_all <- (p_eff_body + p_mess_body) / (p_eff + p_mess)
print(p_combined_all)
```

### TPD

```{r}
#| label: analysis-slope-showdown
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
message("Running Slope Comparison (Aggregated Model)...")
aggregated_data <- pollen_deposition %>%
    group_by(year, plant_id) %>%
    summarise(
        mean_HP = mean(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE),
        mean_CP = mean(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE),
        .groups = "drop"
    ) %>%
    filter(is.finite(mean_HP) & is.finite(mean_CP)) %>%
    mutate(Year = as.factor(year))

# Model on Sqrt Scale (as validated in S4)
fit_sqrt <- lm(sqrt(mean_HP) ~ sqrt(mean_CP) * Year, data = aggregated_data)

# ANOVA results

# Pairwise Compariasons of Slopes
slopes_em <- emtrends(fit_sqrt, pairwise ~ Year, var = "mean_CP")

# Visualisation as it will appear in the MS (backtransformed lines + predictions)
message("Generating Backtransformed Plot for Aggregated Data...")

# Create grid for predictions
pred_grid <- expand.grid(
    mean_CP = seq(min(aggregated_data$mean_CP, na.rm = TRUE),
        max(aggregated_data$mean_CP, na.rm = TRUE),
        length.out = 100
    ),
    Year = levels(aggregated_data$Year)
)

# Predict on the sqrt scale and backtransform
preds <- predict(fit_sqrt, newdata = pred_grid, se.fit = TRUE)
pred_grid$pred_sqrt_HP <- preds$fit
pred_grid$se_sqrt_HP <- preds$se.fit

pred_grid$pred_HP <- pred_grid$pred_sqrt_HP^2 # Backtransformation
# approximate 95% CI: +/- 1.96 * SE, clamped at 0 for lower bound before squaring
pred_grid$lwr_HP <- pmax(0, pred_grid$pred_sqrt_HP - 1.96 * pred_grid$se_sqrt_HP)^2
pred_grid$upr_HP <- (pred_grid$pred_sqrt_HP + 1.96 * pred_grid$se_sqrt_HP)^2

# Create plot
p_model <- ggplot() +
    geom_ribbon(data = pred_grid, aes(x = mean_CP, ymin = lwr_HP, ymax = upr_HP, fill = Year), alpha = 0.2) +
    geom_point(data = aggregated_data, aes(x = mean_CP, y = mean_HP, color = Year), alpha = 0.6, size = 2) +
    geom_line(data = pred_grid, aes(x = mean_CP, y = pred_HP, color = Year), linewidth = 1.2) +
    geom_abline(intercept = 0, slope = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "gray50", linewidth = 0.8) +
    scale_color_viridis_d(begin = 0.2, end = 0.8, option = "D", name = "Year") +
    scale_fill_viridis_d(begin = 0.2, end = 0.8, option = "D", name = "Year") +
    labs(
        x = "Conspecific Pollen (mean per plant)",
        y = "Heterospecific Pollen (mean per plant)"
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        legend.position = "right"
    )
# p_model saved to be combined with variance panel later

# Table for Manuscript (ANOVA Results & Pairwise Slope Contrasts)
message("Generating Combined Model Results Table...")

# A) ANOVA Part
aov_res <- as.data.frame(anova(fit_sqrt))
aov_part <- data.frame(
    Term = gsub("sqrt\\(mean_CP\\)", "Conspecific Pollen (CP)", rownames(aov_res)),
    Df = as.character(aov_res$Df),
    Stat1 = sprintf("%.3f", aov_res$`Sum Sq`),
    Stat2 = sprintf("%.3f", aov_res$`Mean Sq`),
    Stat3 = sprintf("%.2f", aov_res$`F value`),
    P_value = format.pval(aov_res$`Pr(>F)`, eps = 0.001, digits = 3),
    stringsAsFactors = FALSE
)
aov_part[is.na(aov_part) | aov_part == "NA"] <- ""

aov_part %>%
    gt() %>%
    tab_header(
        title = md("**A) ANOVA Results**")
    ) %>%
    cols_label(
        Term = "Predictor",
        Df = "df",
        Stat1 = "Sum Sq",
        Stat2 = "Mean Sq",
        Stat3 = "F-value",
        P_value = "P-value"
    ) %>%
    tab_style(
        style = cell_text(weight = "bold"),
        locations = cells_column_labels()
    ) %>%
    tab_options(
        column_labels.background.color = "#F0F0F0",
        table.border.top.color = "black",
        table.border.top.width = px(2),
        table.border.bottom.color = "black",
        table.border.bottom.width = px(2),
        data_row.padding = px(6)
    ) %>%
    cols_align(align = "center", columns = c(Df, Stat1, Stat2, Stat3, P_value)) %>%
    cols_align(align = "left", columns = Term) %>%
    opt_align_table_header(align = "left")

# B) Contrasts Part
contrasts_res <- as.data.frame(slopes_em$contrasts)
contrasts_part <- data.frame(
    Term = contrasts_res$contrast,
    Df = as.character(round(contrasts_res$df, 1)),
    Stat1 = sprintf("%.3f", contrasts_res$estimate),
    Stat2 = sprintf("%.3f", contrasts_res$SE),
    Stat3 = sprintf("%.2f", contrasts_res$t.ratio),
    P_value = format.pval(contrasts_res$p.value, eps = 0.001, digits = 3),
    stringsAsFactors = FALSE
)
contrasts_part[is.na(contrasts_part) | contrasts_part == "NA"] <- ""

contrasts_part %>%
    gt() %>%
    tab_header(
        title = md("**B) Pairwise Slope Contrasts**")
    ) %>%
    cols_label(
        Term = "Contrast",
        Df = "df",
        Stat1 = "Estimate",
        Stat2 = "SE",
        Stat3 = "t-ratio",
        P_value = "P-value"
    ) %>%
    tab_style(
        style = cell_text(weight = "bold"),
        locations = cells_column_labels()
    ) %>%
    tab_options(
        column_labels.background.color = "#F0F0F0",
        table.border.top.color = "black",
        table.border.top.width = px(2),
        table.border.bottom.color = "black",
        table.border.bottom.width = px(2),
        data_row.padding = px(6)
    ) %>%
    cols_align(align = "center", columns = c(Df, Stat1, Stat2, Stat3, P_value)) %>%
    cols_align(align = "left", columns = Term) %>%
    opt_align_table_header(align = "left")
```

In total, we collected 693 stigmas from 273 individual plants in (84, 89, 520 stigmas in 2022, 2023 and 2024 respectively).

### Coefficient of Variation and Variance Partitioning

To quantify the reduction of unpredictability across the pollen transport network, we compared the Coefficient of Variation (CV) across functional transport compartments. Conspecific pollen (CP) exhibited extreme variability on pollinator bodies (CV = **\[X\]**%) and during single-visit depositions (CV = **\[X\]**%). However, at the terminal depositional stage on final stigmas, this variability was heavily buffered, dropping dramatically (CV = **\[X\]**%). Conversely, heterospecific pollen (HP) maintained extreme hyper-variability across all compartments, from vector bodies (CV = **\[X\]**%) through to final stigmatic loads (CV = **\[X\]**%).

Hierarchical variance partitioning further confirmed the micro-scale nature of this stochasticity. For CP, temporal phenology (years and days) explained negligible variation; instead, the vast majority of depositional variance occurred at the micro-spatial scale, driven by differences among individual plants (**\[X\]**%) and individual stigmas (**\[X\]**%). The reduction in CP variance at the terminal stage, despite intense micro-scale unpredictability during transport, indicates a functional filtering mechanism stabilizing conspecific reproductive receipt. Notably, the variance explained by day in the 2024 dataset was negligible.

```{r}
#| label: analysis-cv-comparison
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| fig-cap: "Coefficient of Variation (CV) and Mean of Conspecific (CP) and Heterospecific (HP) pollen counts. A-B) CV of pollen counts; C-D) Mean pollen counts + SE."
#| fig-height: 8
# 1. Stigma
cv_stigma <- pollen_deposition %>%
    summarise(
        CP = sd(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / mean(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) * 100,
        HP = sd(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / mean(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) * 100
    ) %>%
    mutate(Compartment = "Stigma")

mean_se_stigma <- pollen_deposition %>%
    summarise(
        CP_mean = mean(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE),
        CP_se = sd(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(sum(!is.na(conspecific_pollen))),
        HP_mean = mean(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE),
        HP_se = sd(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(sum(!is.na(heterospecific_pollen)))
    ) %>%
    mutate(Compartment = "Stigma")

# 2. Body
if (!exists("pollen_load_guts_24")) {
    data("pollen_load_guts_24", package = "HanPolNet")
}

cv_body <- pollen_load_guts_24 %>%
    summarise(
        CP = sd(suc_pra_load, na.rm = TRUE) / mean(suc_pra_load, na.rm = TRUE) * 100,
        HP = sd(other_pollen_load, na.rm = TRUE) / mean(other_pollen_load, na.rm = TRUE) * 100
    ) %>%
    mutate(Compartment = "Body")

mean_se_body <- pollen_load_guts_24 %>%
    summarise(
        CP_mean = mean(suc_pra_load, na.rm = TRUE),
        CP_se = sd(suc_pra_load, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(sum(!is.na(suc_pra_load))),
        HP_mean = mean(other_pollen_load, na.rm = TRUE),
        HP_se = sd(other_pollen_load, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(sum(!is.na(other_pollen_load)))
    ) %>%
    mutate(Compartment = "Body")

# 3. SVD
cv_SVD <- SVD_Succisa_22_24 %>%
    summarise(
        CP = sd(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / mean(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) * 100,
        HP = sd(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / mean(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) * 100
    ) %>%
    mutate(Compartment = "SVD")

mean_se_SVD <- SVD_Succisa_22_24 %>%
    summarise(
        CP_mean = mean(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE),
        CP_se = sd(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(sum(!is.na(conspecific_pollen))),
        HP_mean = mean(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE),
        HP_se = sd(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(sum(!is.na(heterospecific_pollen)))
    ) %>%
    mutate(Compartment = "SVD")

# Combine and Reshape CV
cv_data <- bind_rows(cv_stigma, cv_body, cv_SVD) %>%
    pivot_longer(cols = c(CP, HP), names_to = "Pollen_Type", values_to = "CV")

# Combine and Reshape Mean/SE
mean_se_data_cp <- bind_rows(mean_se_stigma, mean_se_body, mean_se_SVD) %>%
    select(Compartment, mean = CP_mean, se = CP_se) %>%
    mutate(Pollen_Type = "CP")

mean_se_data_hp <- bind_rows(mean_se_stigma, mean_se_body, mean_se_SVD) %>%
    select(Compartment, mean = HP_mean, se = HP_se) %>%
    mutate(Pollen_Type = "HP")

mean_se_data <- bind_rows(mean_se_data_cp, mean_se_data_hp)

# CV by year (Stigma)
cv_by_year <- pollen_deposition %>%
    filter(!is.na(year)) %>%
    group_by(year) %>%
    summarise(
        CP = sd(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / mean(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) * 100,
        HP = sd(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / mean(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) * 100,
        .groups = "drop"
    ) %>%
    pivot_longer(cols = c(CP, HP), names_to = "Pollen_Type", values_to = "CV") %>%
    mutate(Year = factor(paste0("20", year)))

# Mean/SE by year (Stigma)
mean_se_by_year_raw <- pollen_deposition %>%
    filter(!is.na(year)) %>%
    group_by(year) %>%
    summarise(
        CP_mean = mean(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE),
        CP_se = sd(conspecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(sum(!is.na(conspecific_pollen))),
        HP_mean = mean(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE),
        HP_se = sd(heterospecific_pollen, na.rm = TRUE) / sqrt(sum(!is.na(heterospecific_pollen))),
        .groups = "drop"
    ) %>%
    mutate(Year = factor(paste0("20", year)))

mean_se_year_cp <- mean_se_by_year_raw %>%
    select(Year, mean = CP_mean, se = CP_se) %>%
    mutate(Pollen_Type = "CP")

mean_se_year_hp <- mean_se_by_year_raw %>%
    select(Year, mean = HP_mean, se = HP_se) %>%
    mutate(Pollen_Type = "HP")

mean_se_by_year <- bind_rows(mean_se_year_cp, mean_se_year_hp)

# Determine shared Y-axis limit for CV
max_cv <- max(c(cv_data$CV, cv_by_year$CV), na.rm = TRUE)
y_limit_max <- max_cv * 1.15

# Set Factor Levels
comp_levels <- c("Body", "SVD", "Stigma")
cv_data$Compartment <- factor(cv_data$Compartment, levels = comp_levels)
mean_se_data$Compartment <- factor(mean_se_data$Compartment, levels = comp_levels)

# Plot A: Compartments CV
p_cv <- ggplot(cv_data, aes(x = Compartment, y = CV, fill = Pollen_Type)) +
    geom_bar(stat = "identity", position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), color = "black", width = 0.8) +
    geom_text(aes(label = round(CV, 1)), position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), vjust = -0.5, size = 4) +
    scale_fill_manual(
        values = c("CP" = "#1ABC9C", "HP" = "#F1948A"),
        labels = c("CP" = "Conspecific", "HP" = "Heterospecific")
    ) +
    scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0, y_limit_max)) +
    labs(
        title = "A) Compartments (CV)",
        y = "CV (%)",
        x = "",
        fill = "Pollen Type"
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        axis.text.x = element_text(size = 12, face = "bold"),
        axis.text.y = element_text(size = 12),
        legend.position = "none"
    )

# Plot B: Stigma per year CV
p_cv_year <- ggplot(cv_by_year, aes(x = Year, y = CV, fill = Pollen_Type)) +
    geom_bar(stat = "identity", position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), color = "black", width = 0.8) +
    geom_text(aes(label = round(CV, 1)), position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), vjust = -0.5, size = 4) +
    scale_fill_manual(
        values = c("CP" = "#1ABC9C", "HP" = "#F1948A"),
        labels = c("CP" = "Conspecific", "HP" = "Heterospecific")
    ) +
    scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0, y_limit_max)) +
    labs(
        title = "B) Stigma per Year (CV)",
        y = "",
        x = "",
        fill = "Pollen Type"
    ) +
    theme_classic() +
    theme(
        axis.text.x = element_text(size = 12, face = "bold"),
        axis.text.y = element_text(size = 12),
        legend.position = "none"
    )

# Plot C: Compartments Mean + SE
p_mean_comp <- ggplot(mean_se_data, aes(x = Compartment, y = mean, fill = Pollen_Type)) +
    geom_bar(stat = "identity", position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), color = "black", width = 0.8) +
    geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = pmax(0, mean - se), ymax = mean + se), position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), width = 0.25) +
    scale_fill_manual(
